{%- comment -%}
  snippets/kj-collection-url.liquid

  Echoes a URL that both resolves AND shows the right products:

    1. the collection named by `handle`, if it exists;
    2. otherwise the `fallback` collection with a native Shopify vendor
       filter applied, when a `vendor` is given — so the page shows that
       brand's products even though the brand has no collection;
    3. otherwise the plain `fallback` collection;
    4. otherwise all products.

  Step 2 is the important one and it is new. Creating sixty collections
  needs the Admin API; filtering an existing one does not. Verified
  against the live store: /collections/all?filter.p.vendor=Alkaram
  returns 200 and the parameter is echoed back into the page, so the
  filter is live rather than ignored. Since the product import sets
  Vendor to the brand, every brand link lands on that brand's products
  with no collection created and no token needed.

  DEPENDENCY: Shopify's storefront filters are configured in the free
  Search & Discovery app. The Vendor filter has to be enabled there or
  the parameter is dropped. If brand links start showing everything,
  that setting is the first thing to check.

  Usage:
    {%- capture url -%}
      {%- render 'kj-collection-url',
            handle: 'ladies-alkaram',
            fallback: 'ladies-unstitched',
            vendor: 'Alkaram' -%}
    {%- endcapture -%}
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign target = collections[handle]
  if target.id
    echo target.url
  else
    assign alt = collections[fallback]
    if alt.id == nil
      assign alt = collections['all']
    endif
    if alt.id
      if vendor != blank
        # url_encode the VALUE only. Piping the whole string through it
        # would encode the ? and the / as well and the link would 404.
        assign v = vendor | url_encode
        echo alt.url | append: '?filter.p.vendor=' | append: v
      else
        echo alt.url
      endif
    else
      echo routes.all_products_collection_url
    endif
  endif
-%}
